\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Moving a label in its orbit}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let a group #{G} act on a set #{X}, let #{r\in X}, and write
#{G_r=\{g:gr=r\}} for its stabilizer. The map #{gG_r\mapsto gr}
is a bijection from the left cosets of #{G_r} onto the orbit #{Gr}.
At every point #{br} of this orbit, #{G_{br}=bG_rb^{-1}}.
If #{G} is finite, then
##{|Gr|\,|G_r|=|G|.}
For the \vocabk{orbit of a momentum label}{fdip-0164} these formulas
use #{G=B_\Lambda} and #{G_r=B_{\Lambda,r}}.}

\proof{
\p{Two elements #{g,h} have the same image of #{r} precisely when
#{h^{-1}g} fixes #{r}. That condition is equivalent to
#{gG_r=hG_r}. Hence the map on cosets is well defined and
injective, and every point in the orbit is an image. Each left
coset has cardinality #{|G_r|} by left multiplication, yielding
the count for finite #{G}. Finally #{gbr=br} holds precisely when
#{b^{-1}gb} fixes #{r}; taking all such #{g} gives the conjugacy
formula.}
}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{MulAction.orbitEquivQuotientStabilizer}
gives the inverse bijection, from the orbit to the cosets.
Its finite cardinality formula is
\leanref{MulAction.card_orbit_mul_card_stabilizer_eq_card_group}.
Conjugacy of the two fixing subgroups is
\leanref{MulAction.stabilizer_smul_eq_stabilizer_map_conj}.}
